WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.860
Welcome to Fascinating Frontiers, Episode 79. Today is May 24th, 2026. Some fascinating developments

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:16.780
to cover today. Looking back at six episodes from 2026 May 18th to 2026 May 24th. The stories

00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:23.480
that mattered, what we learned and what to watch next. Verizon, AT&amp;T, and T-Mobile are jointly

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:29.140
developing direct to device satellite service for the first time. The three carriers have formed

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:34.320
this alliance specifically to offer satellite connectivity directly to phones and other devices.

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:40.000
The partnership directly responds to Starlink's expanding coverage, threatening traditional

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:45.980
networks. Company statements note that ground-based networks face growing competition from orbital

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:52.100
systems already providing widespread coverage. From corporate strategy on the ground to the vehicles

00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:58.560
that will actually fly those signals, we turn to the latest Starship Test Timeline. SpaceX has

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:04.580
set a new no earlier than date of Thursday for the next integrated flight test. The target date

00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:11.140
for Starship Flight 12 is now May 21. The mission will focus on heat shield performance, engine

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:18.220
reliability, and landing system data. Engineers will collect measurements on tile durability during reentry

00:01:18.220 --> 00:01:24.720
and on engine startup sequences after prior test flights. Data from the booster landing attempt

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:30.440
and ships flashed down while in form future vehicle iterations. While private rockets push hardware

00:01:30.440 --> 00:01:35.740
boundaries, NASA is installing new leadership to steer the broader human spaceflight effort.

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:42.600
Matt Anderson took the oath at the Eisenhower Executive Office Building. He was sworn in as NASA's

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:49.080
16th deputy administrator during the formal ceremony. His portfolio includes scientific discovery,

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:55.900
US Space Leadership, and the Artemis Return to the Moon. Anderson will support agency efforts on

00:01:55.900 --> 00:02:01.460
lunar landing timelines and international partnerships tied to the program. With fresh leadership

00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:06.740
in place, it's worth noting how other nations are already being recognized for lunar success.

00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:13.340
India's Chandrian 3 mission has been recognized with the top International Space Award

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:19.380
for its successful lunar-self pole landing. The mission earned the prize for achieving the first

00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:25.520
successful landing near the lunar-self pole. Precise navigation under challenging conditions allowed

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:31.500
the spacecraft to touchdown safely in the targeted region. Organizers highlighted the engineering

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:37.400
contributions that advanced lunar exploration capabilities for future missions. Recognition for past

00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:42.500
landing pairs naturally with new thinking about how we search for life on other worlds.

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:48.340
Earlier hopes for clear atmospheric signals have led researchers to adjust target selection

00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:54.220
and observing methods for the James Webb Space Telescope. The revised plan emphasizes different

00:02:54.220 --> 00:03:00.220
spectral features to improve detection chances. Astronomers are now prioritizing planets with

00:03:00.220 --> 00:03:05.520
specific atmospheric compositions that may produce stronger signals in certain wavelength bands.

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:11.500
The updated strategy includes changes in exposure times and filter selections during transit

00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:17.220
observations. Rethinking distant observations brings us back to practical questions of living

00:03:17.220 --> 00:03:22.620
on another planet. Beneficial fungi are being evaluated for their ability to break down

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:28.560
regolith and support crop growth on Mars. Researchers are testing how these microbes interact

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:34.600
with the mineral-heavy soil in controlled experiments. The goal is to reduce the mass of food supplies,

00:03:34.600 --> 00:03:40.700
future crews must carry from Earth. Early trials examine whether repeated crop cycles can be

00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:47.140
sustained using the treated regolith. Growing food on Mars is one long-term challenge, keeping

00:03:47.140 --> 00:03:52.840
crews healthy during the journey is another. British Olympic rover Helen Glover used a

00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:59.060
parabolic flight to trial a rowing-based resistance machine for future stations. The compact exercise

00:03:59.060 --> 00:04:05.220
device was tested in short periods of weightlessness aboard the aircraft. Engineers collected data on

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:11.120
whether the motion provides effective training without gravity. Measurements focused on force output

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:17.360
and muscle engagement during the rowing strokes. From the human body in space, we shift to bodies of work

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:26.520
already honored on Earth. Tom Acres and Joe Tanner were recognized at Kennedy Space Center for their

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:31.800
shuttle veterans were inducted into the U.S. Astronaut Hall of Fame during the ceremony. Their

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:38.080
contributions include multiple flights and numerous extra vehicular activities that supported NASA's

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:44.320
human spaceflight program. The event highlighted decades of service advancing spacecraft operations

00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:50.660
and assembly tasks in orbit. Before we go, keep an eye on the next round of Starship Test Data

00:04:50.660 --> 00:04:56.500
and any updates from the new NASA leadership on Artemis timelines. That covers today's

00:04:56.500 --> 00:05:01.620
space and science news. Share this with a fellow space enthusiast if you found it interesting.

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:08.220
I'm Patrick in Vancouver. See you tomorrow. This episode used AI voice synthesis of my voice.

00:05:08.860 --> 00:05:11.260
Editorial selection and analysis are my own.
